boringca (5.1.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New uninstall-trust command, the reverse of install-trust: removes the
    store's CA from the system trust store (sudo rm + refresh) and from the
    Firefox/Chromium NSS databases, leaving the CA itself in --dir. Entries
    are found by the store's unique name, so it still works once the store
    directory is gone (except on Arch, where p11-kit needs ca.crt); entries
    of other stores are never touched.
  * Bash completion, man page and README updated accordingly.

 -- Fabrice Dagorn <fabrice@dagorn.fr>  Wed, 07 Oct 2026 10:21:12 +0200

boringca (5.0.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Security: private keys are created with mode 600 from the start, and
    keys/certificates are written atomically (temporary file + rename).
  * issue: refuse to overwrite an existing certificate without --force.
  * issue: default SAN is ip:<cn> for an IP address and dns:<cn> for a host
    name; an invalid host name is an error for server certs and gives no
    SAN for client certs. Explicit dns: SANs are validated.
  * issue: validity is exactly --days (825 days by default, was 826) and
    capped to the CA's expiry; --days 0 or too large is rejected.
  * issue: leaf certificates no longer carry keyEncipherment (ECDSA).
  * issue: the CA's CN is read from ca.crt and checked against ca.key;
    ca.cn is now informational only.
  * init: new CAs are created with pathlen:0.
  * install-trust: detect the mechanism from the trust anchors directory
    (fixes Arch, adds openSUSE); per-store unique file names and NSS
    nicknames (migrating entries from older versions); replace a previous
    CA from the same store in browsers; look up tools in sbin; sudo/doas
    fallback; Firefox Snap/Flatpak and Chromium Snap support.
  * CLI: "help" subcommand, -h/--help after subcommands, --opt=value, "--",
    unexpected arguments and conflicting --server/--client/--both rejected;
    validation happens before the CA is auto-created.
  * Unit tests (cargo test).

 -- Fabrice Dagorn <fabrice@dagorn.fr>  Mon, 05 Oct 2026 13:19:20 +0200

